// @check
// @error 'e' was narrowed by a guard, but the assignment on line 21 ended the narrowing
// CONTRACT (must-error): THE SOUNDNESS BOUNDARY OF THE LOOP-BODY NARROWING, and it is at
// the READ, not the write.
//
// A `while e != null { … }` body reads `e` narrowed. Writing a null back is LEGAL — what
// a place accepts is its storage type, and `e: i32 | null` accepts null — and the write
// RETIRES the narrowing, so a read after it is what has to be refused. The `while` guard
// is re-tested on every back edge, so the next iteration re-derives the fact; only the
// statements between the write and the loop's end lose it, and `e + 1` is one of them.
//
// This is not a rule invented for loops. The `if` arm behaves identically (`if e != null {
// e = null   e + 1 }` is the same diagnostic), and the accepted order — read, THEN write —
// is `soundness/narrowed-write-retires-bare-name.vl`, where the same body walks a nullable
// chain to exhaustion.
//
// A change that accepts the write without retiring the narrowing turns this file green,
// and the program it green-lights adds `1` to a null.
function f(e: i32 | null) {
  while e != null {
    e = null
    return e + 1
  }
  return 0
}
print(toString(f(5)))
